## What this crate is

The declared signature, and nothing else. A declared signature is a format paired with an adaptation,
and it is what every operation arvo declares is a function of. The carrier is not here and cannot be
reached from here, because this crate does not depend on the crate that has one.

That absence is the design rather than an omission. The floor says every operation the design declares
is a function of the declared width and never of the machine carrier, and a dependency edge is the
only way to state that so it cannot quietly stop being true.

## The representable set is one predicate, not a family of them

A format is identified by its ambient domain and its representable set, and the representable set is a
constant of the type. Membership is one affine predicate over one parameterisation: a slot function, a
quantum per magnitude, and a phase.

For a value `v` in the ambient domain, `v` is representable exactly when there is a magnitude `m` and
a slot `i` within the slot range at `m` such that

```
v = phase + i * quantum(m)
```

Integers, fixed point, scaled integers and floats are points of that one predicate rather than four
concepts:

| Point | Quantum | Phase | Magnitudes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Integers | `1`, constant | zero | one |
| Fixed point at fraction width `F` | `radix^-F`, constant | zero | one |
| Scaled integers at scale `s` | `s`, constant | zero, or nonzero when biased | one |
| Floats | `radix^(m - precision + 1)`, indexed by magnitude | zero | many |

Subnormals are not a case. They fall out of taking the maximum at the smallest magnitude, which is
what the rule above says and is the test that the unification is real rather than a presentation.

**The phase is carried explicitly and is never assumed zero.** What it decides is whether the set
carries an additive identity, and that question is the membership predicate above asked at the value
zero rather than a separate rule. Zero is in the set exactly when some admitted magnitude and some
admitted slot cancel the phase, so the existential runs over both coordinates, the same two the
predicate quantifies over.

Written out, `phase + i * quantum(m) = 0`. The phase is a rational `n / d` in units of the quantum at
magnitude zero and the quantum is an affine power law, so substituting
`phase = (n / d) * radix^BASE` and `quantum(m) = radix^(BASE + SLOPE * m)`, dividing through by
`radix^BASE` and clearing the denominator leaves

```
i * d * radix^(SLOPE * m) = -n
```

**The intercept leaves the problem entirely.** `BASE` appears on neither side, so moving it alone
moves no answer at any magnitude, which is a claim about the arithmetic a reader can check rather than
a description of it. The power scales whichever side keeps both whole, and a cancelling slot exists at
a magnitude exactly when that division is exact and its quotient is a value a slot index carries.

Two consequences follow that the whole-multiple property alone does not carry. A whole phase whose
cancelling slot falls outside the slot range leaves no identity on the grid. And where the quantum
shrinks as the magnitude rises, a phase with a fractional part at one magnitude is a whole number of
steps at a higher one, so it does leave the identity on the grid.

**In the constant family this reduces to the whole-multiple rule**, because there is one quantum and
one magnitude, and that is the family the reduction was measured in: it is stated over a fraction
width, which is that family's exponent. It does not reduce that way in the magnitude-indexed family,
where "the quantum" names a different value at each magnitude, so a design
stating the reduction as the deciding property has stated a constant-family fact about both families.

A half-step grid is the case that carries no identity in either family: it is not closed under exact
addition, it holds neither zero nor one when its quantum is one, and it is not even a monoid carrier.
A design that hardcodes the phase to zero has closed that case silently, which is why the coordinate is
present even though every point in the table above except one biased case sets it to zero.

**The phase coordinate holds the pair it was declared with.** `Phase` carries a numerator and a
denominator rather than a normalised ratio, because normalising to a positive denominator is not
always possible in the width the coordinates are declared in and nothing needs it: divisibility and
the quotient are both independent of the denominator's sign, and no other reader consults it. Every
computation over the pair is taken one domain wider than the coordinates, so the two pairs that have
no normalisation are answered rather than reinterpreted.

## The quantum law is where the two families differ, and it is the only place

`Quantum` is the axis that separates the constant-quantum family from the magnitude-indexed one. Every
other coordinate is shared. So a claim about one family is a claim predicated on the quantum law, and
the crate carries that as a named axis rather than as two parallel hierarchies.

This is what makes the cross-kind arithmetic question a question about one space rather than about an
interface between two. It is also where that space is known to be awkward: within one kind the points
form a lattice, and putting both kinds in one space breaks the join on pairs that are both
fixed-point, because a floating point can sit between two fixed points and be incomparable with the
one that used to be the join. That is a measured result, and whether the join is closed across the two
kinds or has to be paid for is not settled. **This crate does not close it and does not depend on it
being closed**: nothing here needs a join.

## The adaptation is a first-class object

Arithmetic on a format is an exact operation in the ambient domain composed with a named, total
adaptation onto the representable set. The adaptation is a thing with its own laws, not a flag on an
operation, and an operation that fuses one invisibly is an operation and an adaptation point wearing
one name.

So the crate names two independent coordinates of an adaptation.

**The rounding mode**, which decides what happens between grid points. Six names, and this is the one
closed enumeration in the design, fixed at these six:

`toward_zero`, `floor`, `ceil`, `half_up`, `half_even`, `stochastic`.

Both spellings of the retired word are gone and do not appear anywhere in this crate. The reason
travels with the vocabulary: on a signed domain that word named two operations that genuinely differ.
Bit truncation of a two's complement value is `floor`, and it is not `toward_zero`; the note exists so
the hardware operation is not read back into the name.

`half_up` is the nearest grid point with a tie going toward positive infinity at every sign, which is
`floor(x + q/2)` for the quantum `q`, and `half_even` is the nearest grid point with a tie going to the
neighbour whose slot is even. A note travels with `half_up` the way the one above travels with the
retired word: it is not Java's or Python's `HALF_UP`, and on a signed domain a tie at `-2.5 q` goes to
`-2 q`. Ties away from zero is a different operation on a signed domain and is not one of the six. The
canon reaches it as an alias, `toward_zero(x + sign(x) q/2)`, a shift of the exact position composed
with the `toward_zero` adaptation, and the two coincide on an unsigned domain and wherever no tie is
representable. Whether a caller can write that alias against this crate is under the open items at the
end, and today the answer is only in part.

**The overflow policy**, which decides what happens outside the representable range. This is an open
inventory, not a closed set. `wrap`, `saturate` and `clamp` are the members the corpus carries;
another joins by supplying the obligations.

The two are independent because they answer different questions, and the realisation map is one map
with two regions rather than two mechanisms: rounding between grid points, completion outside the
range. A magnitude bound switches off the completion and not the rounding, and a grid bound does the
reverse, which is what two regions of one map predict and what two independent mechanisms do not.

### The panic is not an overflow policy

**Two things say so and they agree.** The factoring requires the adaptation to be total. A panic
diverges, so it is not a total map onto the representable set and cannot be a member of the slot.
From the other direction the panic sits on the build axis: it is the go-to for what cannot be caught
at compile time through the typestate, on debug builds, in every
strategy, and on release what stands in its place is the guarantee plus an explicit declaration of the
mode, which lowers and behaves accordingly.

So the panic is a property of how an adaptation is applied under a build profile, and it is not one of
the things an adaptation may be. The build profile is the axis that carries it.

The looseness there is deliberate, so this crate states the shape and does not sharpen it into a rule
about where a panic fires.

## The admission rule, which is how an operation earns its place

The design ships an admission rule rather than an operation list: an operation is admitted exactly
when it is a function of the declared signature, and where two realisations of one name disagree, the
signature is missing a coordinate.

Both halves are load-bearing. The first is the test. The second is what to do when the test fails, and
it says the failure is diagnostic rather than fatal: a name whose two realisations disagree has found
a coordinate nobody declared, and the repair is to declare it.

This is the closed concept and open inventory the format itself has, one level down over operations
rather than over formats.

**What it means for the operation set is that there is not one.** Asking which operations ship is
asking for a list where the rule gives a criterion. What ships is whatever satisfies the rule, and
the standards bound is why that is enough: it is parity in output for the cases a standard covers, it
does not oblige carrying the standard's operation list, and reaching the result by a different or
unconventional route is fine so long as the
logic of the spec is matched.

The worked case is the fused multiply-add. Its result is reachable by composing multiply and add
everywhere except at signed saturating, so it is not a required member of any inventory; it is a
required arm at signed saturating, gated on that region. This crate neither ships that arm nor
forecloses it.

Addition is the instance this crate ships, stated in the last section. It is one member under the
rule, admitted because its exact step reads the phase and the slot range and nothing else, and shipping
it is not the start of a list any more than shipping `Integer` was the start of a list of formats.

## Where the computation lives, and why it is not in a bound

`generic_const_exprs` is forbidden. `min_generic_const_args` is what there is. So a derived quantity
may not be computed in a bound, and every derived quantity is an associated item resolved by an impl.

Read that as the staging boundary rather than as a restriction worked around. The trait is the
stage-zero object: what is known at monomorphisation. A free `const fn` over a format's associated
items is the stage-zero computation. What the backend sees is stage one, and by then every branch the
computation took is gone. Never a runtime check and one lowered path is exactly that, so the contract
form is what the design wants and the const expression in a bound is the thing that would have needed
erasing.

The consequence for anyone implementing this: membership, the quantum at a magnitude, and the slot
range are `const fn`s taking a format as a type parameter and reading its associated items. They are
not methods on a value and they are not bounds.

**A search over the magnitudes is a stage-zero computation like any other.** Deciding whether the set
carries an additive identity means asking the cancellation question at each admitted magnitude, and a
bounded loop inside a `const fn` is how that is written. It is not a runtime check and does not become
one: every input is an associated item fixed at monomorphisation, so the whole search folds to its
answer before the backend sees it.

## What this crate does not decide

Named rather than filled, because the registry holds each open and a design does not close a canon
question as a side effect of choosing a signature.

`does_warm_wrap_or_clamp`, which is a defaults question about a binding and belongs to
`arvo-strategy`'s inventory rather than here. `is_the_derived_numeral_required_to_be_tightest`, where
soundness is stated and bestness is an arm. `does_precision_count_the_sign_digit`.
`does_narrowing_compose` and `what_the_double_rounding_mechanism_is`, both of which bear on composing
adaptations and neither of which the single-adaptation contract here needs.
`is_the_cross_kind_join_closed_or_priced`, above.

`mixed_numeral_addition`, which is why addition takes one signature and not two.
`reduction_order_or_associativity` and `what_a_numeral_guarantees_to_a_fold`, which is why the verdict
on addition's associativity is about the binary operation and says nothing about how a fold may use
it. And a numeral value type carrying a stored slot, which no row licenses and which would be a second
placement beside the one the derivation performs by occupancy, so addition takes and returns slots.

**And how many types the numeric introduction door carries**, which the ruling closing that question
deliberately left open and which the section on the two primitives states in full.

## The laws this crate owes tests for

Stated here because a design that names its laws is what lets the source be checked against something
other than itself, and asserted over the whole matrix of shapes rather than a sample.

**Membership is decidable at const time for every point of the parameterisation**, including a nonzero
phase and a magnitude-indexed quantum.

**The four named points of the table produce the representable sets their conventions document.** This
is the standards bound in its checkable form and it is parity in output.

**Subnormal values fall out of the maximum at the smallest magnitude** with no branch naming them.

**An adaptation is total**: every value of the ambient domain maps to a member of the representable
set, for every rounding mode and every overflow policy. The test that matters is the one that would
fail if a partial member were admitted, which is why the panic is not one.

**Rounding and completion are separable**: a magnitude bound switches off the completion and not the
rounding, and a grid bound does the reverse.

**The additive identity is on the grid exactly when some admitted magnitude and slot cancel the
phase.** The test asserts every direction of that rather than the constant-family reduction of it: a
whole phase whose cancelling slot is in range and one whose cancelling slot is not, a fractional phase
that stays fractional at every admitted magnitude and one that becomes whole at a higher one, and a
format admitting no magnitude at all, whose set is empty and therefore holds nothing.

**And the sweep for it moves the axes the shipped points hold fixed.** The one point carrying a
nonzero phase fixes the denominator at two, the quantum at the constant family and the slots at the
signed range, so a law asserted only through it is a law measured at one point of three axes. The
sweep runs both quantum families, both slot families, denominators other than two, and a radix other
than two, because a law that has only ever been asked one way has not been asked.

**A law over no magnitudes admits nothing, and every function that answers a membership question
agrees about that.** The one that reads only the slot range is the one that got to disagree.

**Each open contract's obligations are refused rather than requested**, over every condition and over
the whole admitted set rather than the shapes this crate ships, with the wrong constructions kept
permanently as tests that report a verdict rather than as constructions somebody deleted.

**Where an obligation is forced decides which tool can see its refusal**, and that is the axis rather
than a property of either tool. An obligation is a const and a const is evaluated where it is used.
Forced from a runtime call it is reached at codegen, which `cargo check` skips, so a doctest, which
builds a binary, is what catches it. Forced in a `const` item it is reached at check time, and a
`trybuild` case both sees it and pins the exact diagnostic, which a `compile_fail` doctest does not.

So both shapes ship and neither is redundant: the doctests cover a declaration reached only through a
running program, and `tests/ui/` covers the const-bound form with its `E0080` committed beside it.

**A test over a declaration reaches something the code does, and there are two ways it fails to.** The
first is an assertion that reaches a declaration and stops: a constant compared against the literal its
own definition set, a few lines up, on a type declared for nothing else. That is a law relating two
declarations to each other and constrains neither, because a wrong declaration produces the same green
as a right one, and it is repaired by running the declaration through the machinery that reads it.

**The second is quieter, and this crate had one of each.** An assertion can be perfectly capable of
failing and still unable to establish the property in its own name, because every construction it
could be written over answers alike under either implementation. No slot range this crate ships
separates a width that is read from one recovered by counting, since the declaration and the bounds
agree on all of them, so no arm over the shipped set could have said which of the two comes back. That
one is repaired by finding a construction on which the two answers differ, and a design naming only
the first failure would send a reader to the wrong repair.

## The surface this design names

The concept is closed and the inventory is open, so this table separates the two.
The contracts are the closed part: a new numeral supplies them and nothing here
grows. The shipped members are instances, and a consumer's own member sits beside
them with no edit to anything below.

| Contract | What an implementor supplies | Shipped members |
|---|---|---|
| `Format` | `Ambient`, `Quantum`, `Slots`, `PHASE`, a `Phase`, and `ADMITTED` | `Integer`, `UFixed`, `Biased`, `Floating`, and the platform-width `USize` and `ISize`, in `points` |
| `Ambient` | `RADIX`, a `Radix`, `SIGNED`, a `Bool`, and `ADMITTED` | `BinaryRationals`, `UnsignedBinaryRationals`, `DecimalRationals` |
| `Quantum` | `BASE` and `SLOPE`, both an `Exponent`, `MAGNITUDES`, a `MagnitudeCount`, the affine step law, and `ADMITTED` | `Constant`, `Indexed` |
| `Slots` | `MIN` and `MAX`, both a `Slot`, inclusive, `WIDTH`, a `Width`, and `ADMITTED` | `Signed`, `Unsigned` |
| `Rounding` | which of the six, as `MODE`, a `Mode` | `TowardZero`, `Floor`, `Ceil`, `HalfUp`, `HalfEven`, `Stochastic` |
| `Overflow` | which policy, as `POLICY`, a `Policy` | `Wrap`, `Saturate`, `Clamp` |
| `Adaptation` | a `Rounding` and an `Overflow` | `Adapt` |
| `DeclaredSignature` | a `Format` and an `Adaptation` | `Signature` |
| `Operation` | the `Signature` it is a function of, and `ARITY`, an `Arity` | `Add`; a consumer's own sits beside it |

**Not one coordinate in that table is a host primitive, and that is the contract rather than a
preference about spelling.** The reason is the section after next.

`ADMITTED` is a default on each of the four contracts that has one, so an implementor supplies it by
supplying coordinates that meet it and never writes it out.

`Mode` and `Policy` are the two value enumerations a const predicate compares
against, and `ALL_MODES` and `SHIPPED_POLICIES` are what a test sweeps so a law is
asserted over the matrix rather than over a remembered subset. `Mode` is closed
at six names; `Policy` is not.

The membership predicate and its neighbours are free functions rather than trait
methods, for the staging reason above: `contains`, `has_additive_identity`,
`cancelling_slot`, `step_exponent`, `smallest_step_exponent`, `radix`,
`exponent_at`, `magnitude_in_range`, `is_constant_family`, `slot_in_range`,
`slot_count`, `rounding_of`, `overflow_of`, `operation_rounding`,
`operation_overflow`, `sum_position`, `add`, `addition_reach`,
`addition_is_associative`. Each takes a coordinate and returns one, and the ones that
answer a yes or no return a `Bool` rather than the host's truth value, on the same
reading as the table above.

**One of those is the predicate asked at a computed position rather than a second reading of the
coordinates.** Membership is one predicate, so whether one particular value is a member is that
predicate at the coordinates denoting it. `has_additive_identity` works out where zero would sit and
asks `contains` about it, and it is written that way because the alternative already happened: a
conjunction over the same coordinates, missing one of them, disagreeing with `contains` about the same
question. **The position is a slot and a magnitude**, and working out only the slot is the way that
conjunction came up short.

`cancelling_slot` is the intermediate that position is built from: the slot that
cancels the phase at one magnitude, if an admitted one does. It is named rather
than inlined because the conjunction and each of its halves are different claims,
and a law over the conjunction alone cannot say which half a failure came from.

Each contract carrying an obligation has the verdict-returning form of it beside
the obligation: `slots::is_admissible`, `is_admissible_ambient`,
`is_admissible_quantum` and `is_admissible_format`. The obligation addition
carries has `is_addable` beside it in the same form.

### The two primitives, and why they are here

`Width` is a count of bits and `Bool` is a truth value. They live in this crate
because it introduces the numeric category, which is the declared door for the one
place the stack's own primitives cannot be written in terms of themselves. Both
are `repr(transparent)` with a single declared unwrap accessor, `count` and `get`,
so the observation surface is exactly what the type establishes and nothing
widens it.

**They are not the bound on what the door carries.** A count of types is not the bound at all.

**What the door carries out is the coordinate set of the parameterisation, spelled in types the stack
owns.** A format's coordinates are what an implementor writes, so a crate outside this one
has to be able to write them, and every one of them is a number or a truth value that the
bare-primitive lints refuse in every crate but this one.

**Deriving them from `Width` and `Bool` was never available.** `Width` is a count of bits over `u32`,
and three of the ten constants the contracts ask for carry values it cannot hold at all: an exponent
is signed, a slot index is wider, and a slot range's lowest index is routinely negative. That is what
makes the recorded option arithmetically impossible rather than merely costly, and it is why a reader
looking for the coordinates to be built out of the two primitives will not find that anywhere.

**How many types the door carries is not settled, and this design does not settle it.** Two readings
are live: a type per coordinate, or more than two and fewer than ten with the signed coordinates
possibly sharing one. **This crate ships one per coordinate**, which is the first of them, and saying
what it ships is a different act from saying what the bound is.

There is a third shape the bound could take, where it stops being a count and becomes a rule about
positions: a bare primitive appears only in the constructor and the accessor of a primitive this
crate itself defines, plus the const generic parameter position that is already excepted. **Named
here as open rather than adopted**, because this design is not where that bound gets chosen.

### The coordinates, and why they are types at all

A format's coordinates are what an implementor writes, so what type each one is
declared with is part of the contract rather than a detail of how this crate
spells it. A design that names the coordinates and never names their types leaves
them as host integers and a host `bool`, and that has a consequence.

**The bare-primitive lints run at error level in every crate, and `arvo-format` is
the only one exempt**, because it introduces the numeric category. So a crate
elsewhere that implements `Format` is refused at the gate for every coordinate it
supplies, and the only way it lands is by turning a lint off. The concept is
closed and the inventory is open: a new instance earns admission by supplying
the concept's obligations, and closing the concept while opening the inventory
is what makes admission a check rather than a negotiation. A contract nobody
outside can supply without editing a lint config has made it a negotiation with
the lint config, which is not the same thing.

**The floor is two, measured rather than argued.** An outside format reusing every
shipped `Ambient`, `Quantum` and `Slots`, and supplying only what `Format` itself
demands, would still write two host integers under a host-typed contract. No
arrangement of the shipped parts gets past the gate, which is what makes it a
defect in the contract rather than an inconvenience at one call site.

An associated const takes any type at all. Only a const generic parameter is
restricted to the host's own, and that position is excepted. So the repair needs
no mechanism: it needs each coordinate to be named for what it is and given a
type that says so.

| Coordinate type | What it is | Declared with |
|---|---|---|
| `Radix` | the base a positional notation counts in | `Ambient` |
| `Exponent` | a signed power of the radix, which is what both the step law's intercept and its rate are | `Quantum` |
| `Magnitude` | an index into a quantum law's magnitudes | `Quantum` |
| `MagnitudeCount` | how many magnitudes a law ranges over | `Quantum` |
| `Slot` | an index of a multiple of the quantum, never a value | `Slots` |
| `SlotCount` | how many slots a range admits | `Slots` |
| `Phase` | the grid's offset, as a ratio in units of the quantum at magnitude zero | `Format` |
| `Arity` | how many operands an operation takes | `Operation` |
| `Fraction` | a ratio with a positive denominator, which is a position between two grid points | the applied map |

**Each lives in the file that declares the contract it is a coordinate of**, rather
than in one coordinates module. One file is one topic, a coordinate is part of the
topic its contract is, and a module gathering all nine would drag every contract
into a reader's view of any one of them.

**An index and a count of the same thing are two types.** `Magnitude` against
`MagnitudeCount`, `Slot` against `SlotCount`. The off-by-one between an index and
an extent is the class this design already worries about where it says the slot
range is inclusive at both ends because the alternative is an error nobody can see
in a const, and carrying both as one host integer is that worry with nothing done
about it.

**The phase is one coordinate and one const.** The parameterisation carries a phase, singular.
`Phase` carries the numerator and the denominator as a pair rather than as one host integer, so the
two halves never have to be unpacked in a const, and it carries the pair as it was written: nothing
normalises the sign and nothing reduces the ratio, so a phase comes back exactly as it went in. Both
questions asked of a phase, whether the ratio is a whole number and which whole number it is, are
independent of the sign of the denominator, and no other reader consults it. The predicates below
are total over the pair, the zero denominator that names no position included.

**So the phase's condition sits on the contract that reads it, not on its
constructor.** A denominator of zero names no position on the grid, `Phase::denotes`
is that predicate written over the coordinate, and `Format::ADMITTED` refuses it
where the coordinates are declared together, which is the shape `Slots`, `Quantum`
and `Ambient` already carry. An invariant a constructor can only hold by discarding
a value it was handed is not an invariant worth holding there.

**The ratio a position carries answers to a different reader, so it does
normalise.** `Fraction` goes to `Exact`, which splits it with a euclidean division
and keeps the remainder over the same denominator, and the `[0, 1)` reading that
split produces is what lets the six rounding modes be stated once rather than once
per sign. A negative denominator there stores a remainder that reads as negative, so
the positive denominator is a property a reader depends on rather than a tidiness the
phase could afford to drop. What that costs, and where it is paid, is under the
applied map below.

Each of these is `repr(transparent)` where it wraps one value, carries a private
field, and has exactly one declared unwrap accessor, on the same reading as `Width`
and `Bool`: a type whose fields are public has not carried the property its
constructors establish.

**Nothing here touches a const generic parameter.** They are excepted, and what
crosses at that surface is unsettled, so this design states its coordinates and
goes no further.

**The compiler refuses that position, and a test says so.**
`an_arvo_type_as_a_const_parameter_is_refused` holds the file that will not build,
so the sentence above is a claim about something that fails loudly rather than a
convention the source happens to follow.

`Width` cannot be written at the position the widths are actually declared in,
which is why `Constant<const EXP: i32>` and `Signed<const BITS: u32>` are spelled
the way they are. A const generic parameter takes an integer, a `bool` or a
`char`, so a transparent newtype over a bit count is refused there for what it
is rather than for what it holds, and the way past that refusal is a compiler
feature. Taking it would put that feature on every crate declaring a format of
its own, which is not a cost to hand downstream, so the parameters stay integers
and `tests/ui/` keeps the refusal where it can be read.

## Applying the adaptation, which is the second half of the composition

The factoring says arithmetic on a format is an exact operation in the ambient domain composed
with a named total adaptation onto the representable set. Naming an adaptation is not applying one, and
without the verb the composition has only its first half.

### The exact position, which is what the map takes

An exact result is stated in the format's own coordinates rather than as a host number, because the map
is a function of the declared signature and a host number is not part of it. A position is a slot and a
remainder within one step:

```
value = phase + (slot + remainder) * quantum(magnitude)
```

`Exact` carries a `Slot` and the remainder as a `Fraction`, a numerator over a denominator, so an
exactly-half case is representable rather than approximated. A remainder of zero is a position already
on the grid, and the map is the identity on it whatever the mode says, which is the first law below.

The `Fraction` a caller hands `Exact::between` may be any ratio at all, and `Exact` normalises it into
the slot, so a caller computing an exact result does not carry the remainder invariant. What `Exact`
holds afterwards is in `[0, 1)`, which is what lets the rounding modes be stated once rather than once
per sign.

The carry that normalisation moves into the slot is at most `2^63` in either direction, and it lands as
an ordinary slot wherever the index's host integer holds the sum. Where it does not, the slot pins at
the integer's end and the constructor keeps, beside it, how far past that end the named slot lies, a
signed count that is zero everywhere else and at most the carry, so it fits the fraction's own
integer. That happens only from a slot within `2^63` of either end, and a range may be admitted there,
`i128::MAX` included. `between(Slot::at(i128::MAX), 9/4)` stores the slot `i128::MAX`, two past it and
a quarter; `between(Slot::at(i128::MIN), -7/4)` stores `i128::MIN`, two below it and a quarter. So
`between` is total over every slot and every ratio and still names the position it was asked for, and
the map answers for that position: under `Ceil` and `Wrap` over a range ending at `i128::MAX` the first
ceils to three past the top and wraps to two above the range's lowest slot, and under `Floor` the second
floors to two below the bottom, out of range, where a range starting at `i128::MIN` wraps it to 254
above its lowest slot. `slot()` answers the pinned slot, which is the end of the index for a position
past it.

Where a position lies past an end, every region reads the pinned slot and the distance together, as
the index section below says, and none of them forms the named slot.

### The ratio coordinate is an adaptation too, one tier down

The format spine describes an operation onto a representable set as an exact operation in the ambient
domain composed with a named total adaptation, and it goes on to require the adaptation to be a
first-class object with its own laws, so that an operation fusing one invisibly is an operation and
an adaptation point wearing a single name.

**The same shape recurs here, and it is a resemblance rather than an instance.** The exact operation
is a ratio in the ambient rationals, the set is the pairs the coordinate carries with a positive
denominator, and the constructor is the map between them. But `Fraction` is not a format, and
`Fraction::of` is one `const fn` doing both halves, so there is no separated adaptation to name and
what the spine says about a format's arithmetic does not carry over to it.

So it has two regions, the same way the applied map below does, and for the same reason.

| Region | What it does | When it applies |
|---|---|---|
| Exact normalisation | returns the pair as given where the denominator is already positive, and otherwise moves the sign to the numerator, cancelling the power of two the operands share where one of them is `i64::MIN` | an exact form exists inside the coordinate |
| Magnitude saturation | the operand that would need `2^63` takes the largest the coordinate carries instead | it does not |

Four properties hold over every pair, and they are what the constructor is:

- The denominator is positive.
- The sign of the answer is the sign of the ratio that was named.
- The answer is exact wherever the coordinate admits an exact form.
- Where it does not, the answer is within a relative `1 / i64::MAX` of the named ratio.

**The second region is one condition, not a list of pairs.** One operand is `i64::MIN`, the
denominator is negative, and the other operand is odd. An odd operand is what leaves nothing to
cancel, and only then does the exact form need a magnitude one past the coordinate. Where the other
operand is even the pair reduces and the answer is exact, so `of(i64::MIN, -2)` is `2^62` over one
rather than a saturated approximation of it.

**Saturation is the crate's own word and this is the same act.** `complete_slot` pins a slot leaving
its range to the bound the declaration carries, and this pins a magnitude leaving the coordinate to
the bound the width carries. Neither refuses, neither panics, and both are total.

**A zero denominator is not one of the two regions.** It names no ratio, so there is nothing for an
answer to be near and nothing lost by reading it as the zero position. That is the same reading the
phase gives it, and the difference is only where the reading is enforced: the phase hands the
question to `Format::ADMITTED` because a phase is declared once in a const, and a fraction is computed
per position by a caller who has no contract to hang it on.

### One map with two regions, not two mechanisms

`adapt` takes an `Exact` and returns a slot the format admits. It has two regions and they are regions
of one map:

| Region | What decides | When it applies |
|---|---|---|
| Between grid points | the `Rounding` mode | the remainder is nonzero |
| Outside the range | the `Overflow` policy | the rounded slot falls outside the slot range |

The order is rounding then completion, and it matters: a position may round onto a slot that is out of
range, so completion sees the rounded slot rather than the exact position. Completion never rounds and
rounding never completes, which is what makes a magnitude bound switch off one and a grid bound the
other.

**Totality is the property the whole factoring rests on.** Every `Exact` maps to a slot in range, for
every mode and every policy, and that is what makes an adaptation a member of the slot the canon
names. It is also why a panic is not a policy.

### The stochastic mode takes its decision as an input

Five of the six modes are a function of the position alone. The sixth is not, and the questions about
how it is seeded and whether it is keyed on value or position are open in the register.

So `adapt` takes a `Dither`, which is a `Fraction` the stochastic mode compares the remainder against,
supplied by the caller. The crate stays a function of its inputs, the mode is expressible, and nothing
here answers the seeding question or the keying question. A caller with no stochastic mode in its
adaptation passes `Dither::UNUSED` and the parameter is erased at monomorphisation with the branch that
reads it.

`adapt` returns a `Slot`, which is the coordinate the format is stated in, and not a host integer that
a caller would then have to know the meaning of.

### The panic is a build-profile behaviour and still not a policy

`panic_on_inexact` and `panic_on_overflow` report whether a debug build should refuse a position rather
than adapt it. Both take the position, and `panic_on_overflow` takes the dither as well, because what it
reports is whether the slot the position rounds to leaves the range, and the rounded slot is what `adapt`
completes. A verdict over a slot the caller had rounded already would need a rounding this crate keeps
internal, so it would be asked about something no caller can produce. They return a verdict rather than
diverging, so the crate itself stays total and the decision to panic belongs to the caller's build.

No operation panics on its own account. `sum_position` is the position addition adapts, so a caller
wanting the debug panic asks the verdict about exactly that position, with the dither it handed `add`.

The bound is stated loosely on purpose. Two concerns bound the panic, and neither of them settles
whether it may appear in dev builds of the speed-first concern. **A design that sharpened this would
be going past the bound rather than completing it.**

### The laws the applied map owes tests for

Each of these can be wrong about arithmetic. A law that reaches a declaration and stops cannot be, which
is the difference and the reason the four below are stated over what the map computes.

**The map is total.** Every position maps to an admitted slot, over every mode, every policy and every
format shape, including positions far outside the range in both directions.

**The map is the identity on the grid.** A position with a zero remainder and a slot in range comes
back unchanged, for every mode and policy. A law naming this without applying the map declares it
rather than checking it, which is the distinction the section above draws.

**Order transport is a property of the applied map.** A policy transports order exactly when the map
never inverts a pair, measured over positions that leave the window in both directions. Wrapping does
not and the other two do, and that is now measured rather than declared, which is what the earlier
`is_monotone` needed.

**Determinism is a property of the applied map.** Five modes give the same slot for one position on
every call regardless of the dither; the sixth does not, and the test varies the dither to show it.
That is what the earlier `is_deterministic` declared about a `matches!` expression.

**Direction is a property of the applied map.** A directed mode moves every off-grid position the same
way relative to zero or to infinity, and that is checkable against the returned slots rather than
against a list of three names.

**The two regions are separable.** A position inside the range with a nonzero remainder exercises
rounding and not completion; a position on the grid and outside the range exercises completion and not
rounding. Both are asserted, because the claim that the map has two regions is a claim that each can be
reached without the other.

The map agrees, at both ends of the index, with an oracle that shares none of its arithmetic. The oracle
holds the rounded position as an integer two limbs wide, so a slot past either end of the index is an
ordinary value to it, and reads neither the pair nor the distance the map itself carries. Its rounding
half computes each mode from the formula that defines it over an exact rational, and never by choosing
between two neighbours the way the map does: `floor` and `ceil` of the position, `toward_zero` as the
sign times the floor of the magnitude, `half_up` as `floor(x + q/2)`, which is adding half a step and
flooring, `half_even` as that with a tie whose result is odd stepped down to the even neighbour, and
the stochastic mode as `ceil(x - d)` at a dither `d` in `[0, 1)`, with a dither below zero read as
`ceil` and one at or above one read as `floor`, which is what `Dither::at` documents. So every tie rule
the oracle applies is the canon's rather than the implementation's. Its completion half carries the one
reading taken from the implementation rather than one the design settles: it reads `Clamp` as
`Saturate`, the collapse the map makes because the declared signature has nowhere to carry a bound, so
the `Clamp` cells check that the map makes that collapse and check nothing about a clamp to a declared
bound.

It is swept over every mode and every policy, over ranges at the top and the bottom of the index, ranges
a few slots in from either end, and ranges at neither end, the 200-slot and `2^64`-slot ones among them,
and each range is fed positions up to eight slots either side of each end of the index and of each end
of the range, at every quarter remainder and under several dithers, with the slot and the overflow
verdict both compared. A second sweep widens the region the first one feeds: distances up to `2^63`
past either end of the index, remainders over the denominators 1, 2, 3, 4, 7 and `i64::MAX`, ranges one
slot wide at zero and at `[5, 5]` and at both ends of the index, the ranges `[0, 2^64 - 1]` and
`[-2^63, 2^63 - 1]`, the `2^64`-slot ranges at both ends of the index, `[-4, 3]`, a 200-slot range off
its own span, `[-100, 99]`, and a 256-slot range, `[1000, 1255]`, and `adapt` and `panic_on_overflow` at
`USize` and `ISize` under every mode and policy. Every broken map
the suite keeps is reported by each of the two sweeps, which is what shows their reach rather than only
their silence over the shipped map.

The broken maps include wrong tie rules as well as wrong completions. The planted ones are a `half_up`
whose tie goes away from zero, which is the rule this crate shipped before the canon settled what
`half_up` denotes, a `half_up` whose tie goes down, one whose tie goes toward zero and one whose tie
goes to the odd neighbour, and a `half_even` whose tie goes to the odd neighbour. Each differs from the
shipped map at a tie only, so a sweep reports it only where it feeds a tie of the sign or the parity at
which that rule differs.

A third sweep is about the ties themselves. Every signed and unsigned format at every admitted width up
to 16 bits is fed every tie from one slot under its range to one slot over it, and every wider one the
ties within sixteen slots of each end of its range and of zero, under every mode and policy, through
`adapt` and `panic_on_overflow`, against the oracle. The ties are written over several denominators,
the largest near the ratio's own integer, so the comparison the map decides a tie by is asked where its
operands are widest. Asked about the planted tie rules through the map's two regions rather than
through `adapt`, the same sweep reports each of them.

**An overflow policy declared outside this crate reaches the map.** The inventory is open, so the test
that means anything runs a policy this crate does not ship through `Adapt`, through `overflow_of` and
through `adapt` itself, and compares the answer against a shipped policy that decides the same way. A
test reading the declared constant back establishes that a constant holds what it was set to.

## The slot range carries the declared width, and where it stops

`Slots` carries three coordinates: the lowest admitted slot index, the highest, and **the declared
width itself**.

The width is a coordinate rather than something recovered from the other two. A declaration of
thirteen bits knows it is thirteen bits, and reconstructing that by counting the slots and taking a
base-two logarithm is a computation over a quantity nobody needs, done at the one place in the chain
where the arithmetic can leave the range it is carried in. **The width is known at declaration, so it
is read rather than derived.**

**A range whose bounds do not determine its width is what says so.** A declaration running from zero to
three and stating thirteen bits reads back as thirteen, and a test asserting that is a test of the
property; a test asserting that a range this crate writes reads back the width the same macro line
wrote is a test of nothing.

### The bound, which is the set of impls rather than a check inside one

The design admits declared widths of 1 through 64 bits, and the reason it stops at 64 is the widest
pointer width a compilation target has and the widest carrier the placement ladder offers, not the
carrier of the index. A slot index and a count of slots are carried as signed 128-bit integers, which
is what reaching 64 takes: the unsigned 64-bit range ends at `2^64 - 1` and both 64-bit ranges count
`2^64` slots, and none of those is a signed 64-bit value. Restating the range as a count or with an
exclusive end, over a signed 64-bit index, reaches 63 and not 64.

Past 64 nothing here is admitted, although the index's host integer would count further. Running the ladder on
to 126, where a count of `2^width` stops fitting a signed 128-bit integer, would answer how wide arvo
goes, which is not settled, and would admit formats the placement ladder has no carrier for.

This crate implements `Slots` at the admitted widths and at no other width. `Signed<65>` has no
impl, so every use of it is a trait-bound error at the use site. There is no guard inside a function,
because a guard is a thing that can be deleted and an absent impl is not.

**The trait itself is open and is not sealed.** A numeral wanting a slot range that is neither of the
two shipped shapes has to be able to supply one, because the concept is closed and the inventory is
open. So the sentence above is about what this crate ships and says nothing about what exists: an
outside crate may implement `Slots`, and the contracts table above lists `Signed` and `Unsigned` as
examples for that reason.

**So do not read the two shipped shapes as the two that can exist.** Nothing enforces such a bound
and nothing could, on a trait an outside crate may implement.

### What an outside implementor owes, and what checks it

The same rule says a new instance earns admission by **supplying the concept's obligations**,
and that closing the concept while opening the inventory is what makes admission **a check rather
than a negotiation**. So the obligations are checked rather than requested.

A slot range is admitted when its lowest index does not exceed its highest, when the width it declares
is one of the admitted widths, and when that width addresses every slot between the two ends. An
implementor supplying an inverted range has not supplied the obligations, and that is refused when a
binary is produced rather than producing a negative count at runtime.

The ends may sit anywhere the index's host integer reaches. A range an outside crate declares is not
bound to the two shipped shapes' neighbourhood of zero, only to its own width, so the span between its
ends is computed with checked arithmetic and a span too wide for that integer to state reads as a width
that does not address it, which is the refusal it is. No separate condition bounds the count: a width of
at most 64 that addresses the span caps the count at `2^64`, which a signed 128-bit count holds with room,
so a condition on the count would be one nothing can fail.

**A quantum law owes two conditions and neither was held by anything.** It ranges over at least one
magnitude, because a law over none describes no values, and the exponent it reaches at its largest
admitted magnitude is one an `Exponent` can carry. Both were stated where nothing could hold them, the
first in a doc comment and the second nowhere at all. The paragraph three below is what condemns the
first: a condition living in a comment is a negotiation, and the point about a check a later edit can
delete is generous here, because there was never a check to delete in the first place.

**The second condition is the slot range's span condition one contract over**, and it is here for the
same reason that one was strengthened. The step law is `radix^(BASE + SLOPE * m)`, so a law declaring
a rate and a count that between them run the exponent past what it carries has not described a
quantum at every magnitude it claims. The check is done in a domain wide enough to hold the product,
so the obligation cannot overflow while checking that the thing it is about does not. The slot range's
span condition gets the same guarantee a different way, by computing the span checked and reading an
overflow as the refusal.

**What the absence cost is worth naming, since it is what makes the addition more than tidiness.**
`contains` conjoins the magnitude range, so under a law over no magnitudes nothing is a member.
`has_additive_identity` read the slot range and not the magnitude range, and so answered that the
identity is present. Two functions in one file disagreed about whether zero is in the set, and the one
that disagreed with `contains` is the one that was not derived from it.

**Exactly where, because a guarantee that names no site is not checkable and an unqualified claim here
would repeat the defect this section exists to fix.** Each obligation is a const, so it is evaluated
where it is used and nowhere else, and the guarantee is exactly the set of functions that use it.
`Format::ADMITTED` is forced by two functions, `cancelling_slot` and `has_additive_identity`, and by
nothing else in the crate. `Ambient`, `Quantum` and `Slots` each have their own forcing sets, which are
different sets rather than the same two: `Slots::ADMITTED` is forced by the slot range's own accessors
and by `adapt`, which is where a value is put back on the grid.

**So the guarantee is over those verbs and not over the declaration.** A declaration that no forcing
verb is ever asked about is refused nowhere, which follows from an associated const being evaluated at
its use rather than at its definition, and no forcing site can close it. Two routes reach a value
without meeting it: reading a coordinate off the impl, and `contains`, which is this crate's own
spelling of the membership predicate. `contains` carries no force site of its own: it is
`magnitude_in_range::<F::Quantum>(magnitude).and(slot_in_range::<F::Slots>(slot))`, so it reaches the
quantum's obligation and the slot range's through those two calls, and it reaches neither the format's
own nor the ambient's, which are the two this section is about.

**A third route passes a forcing site and defeats it there, which is a different thing and wants
saying separately.** An implementor writing an empty `ADMITTED` over the default is not missing the
site: `has_additive_identity` evaluates whatever that implementor supplied, and what it supplied
imposes nothing. So the guarantee over those verbs holds for an implementor that leaves the default in
place, and an implementor is free not to. Closing the first two routes would need a verb that forces;
closing this one needs the obligation to be something an implementor cannot write over.

Whether the design closes those routes is open, since the canon does not choose the shape a law takes.
What is settled is that the sentence names its extension: an obligation reaches what asks for it.

The refusal reaches implementors this crate cannot see, which is what makes it a check on the concept
rather than on the two shapes shipped with it.

**What it does not do is catch an incoherence no obligation names.** It refuses what the obligations
state and nothing further, so the obligations are the design decision and the mechanism is only how
they are enforced.

**Each obligation has a verdict beside it that asks the same question without forcing the const.** A
construction that compiles and is wrong can then be reported on rather than refusing the build, which
is what lets the wrong construction live permanently in a test instead of in a scratch file somebody
deletes.

`#[diagnostic::on_unimplemented]` carries the message: the bound, and why the bound is where it is. A
reader gets the same sentence a refusal would have given, from a stable attribute rather than a
feature.

The set cannot be widened by accident. An impl computes its own bounds in its own body as shifts of
the 64-bit host integers, the signed ends from `i64::MIN` and `i64::MAX` and the unsigned top from
`u64::MAX`, each shifted right by `64 - width`. At 65 that shift is refused at the definition site, so
adding a width past the bound fails to compile where it was added rather than opening a hole at some use
site later. Written as shifts of the 128-bit host integer instead, the same body would build to 126 and the
bound would have moved into a sentence.

**Why this rather than a checked constructor.** A public function taking a runtime width and asserting
on it is a runtime check on the public surface, which is forbidden, and it is a check a later edit can
remove while every test at admitted widths stays green. The set of impls is the same statement made
where nothing can delete it.

The bound is a statement about what this design admits rather than about how wide arvo goes. How
wide arvo goes in general is unsettled, and this design states what it admits rather than answering
that.

### The index's host integer, and what every computation over a slot owes it

Every function here that reads a slot computes in the slot index's own host integer. There is no wider
domain to reach for, so what keeps each computation exact is headroom inside that integer, and it is
stated per site rather than assumed. The phase and the ratio coordinate are still signed 64-bit pairs,
and the computations over those alone keep their own wider domain, which is the slot index's integer.

Over the ranges this crate ships it is exact with room. Every one sits inside `[-2^63, 2^64 - 1]`,
so two members sum to at most `2^65` and every position addition, the applied map and the reach compute
for members is more than sixty bits inside the host integer.

An outside range can sit near the host integer's end, and there three things hold. A verdict or an
obligation whose own arithmetic could leave the integer computes it checked, and an overflow reads as
the refusal it would have been: the slot range's span, addition's carry condition, and the reflection
comparison that negates the highest slot. A position computed for an operand that is not a member, or
by a chain of carries, saturates at the integer's ends, the act `Reach` and the addition step perform,
so every verb stays total. Those two are the whole list, and each is a position no member reaches: the
carry obligation keeps every range addition admits inside `[-2^126, 2^126)`, so a saturated sum lies
past the same end of the integer as the one named and outside every such range, and the overflow
verdict is right about it. The bound holds at every phase, not only at the zero phase where it is
reached: `sum_is_carried` doubles each end through a checked multiply before adding the phase's whole
part, so the multiply alone caps a range's top at `i128::MAX / 2` and its bottom at `i128::MIN / 2`,
and no phase moves either edge outward from there. What the map answers under wrapping is for the
saturated position, as the section on addition prices.

`Exact::between` is not on that list. A remainder carried past the integer's end pins the slot at the
end and keeps the distance past it, so the position that reaches the map is the one the caller named.

The applied map computes no position that saturates, and the `Saturate` and `Clamp` policies are the
only places it pins anything. They answer alike, since the declared signature has nowhere to carry the
bound a clamp pins to, and the laws section above says what the tests check about that. Rounding answers with the slot at or below the position and
whether to step one above it, and it never forms the slot above, because at `i128::MAX` that slot is one
past the integer. It carries the position's distance past an end beside the pair, and reads the named
slot's sign and parity from the two rather than from the pinned slot. The pair names every position from
the integer's lowest to one past its highest, and the distance names the rest. The completion reads
them as they are: a position past the top is out of every range, and one past the bottom is in range
only where the named slot is one under the integer's lowest, the rounding steps onto it, and the range
starts there; otherwise the position is in range when the slot below is at least the lowest slot or
steps onto it, and at most the highest slot without stepping past it. The wrap reduces the slot below,
the distance and the lowest slot separately, subtracts, adds the step and reduces once more, so every
intermediate is under a small multiple of the span and the answer is exact at every position either
can name; and saturation pins to the end a position past the integer lies beyond, and otherwise to the
lowest slot when the slot below is under it and to the highest when it is not. `panic_on_overflow`
asks the same in-range question of the same pair and distance. So a range placed at the top of the
index adapts a position just under one past its top, or past the integer's end, exactly as a range at
zero does.

### The other three contracts owe the same thing, in the same form

The argument above is about the concept and not about slot ranges. The admission rule is about how
any new instance of any of these concepts earns its place. So the other three open contracts carry
the obligation in the same shape, and what follows is what each one asserts.

**An ambient domain declares a radix of at least two.** A radix below that is not a positional
notation: at one the step never changes with the exponent and every magnitude names the same value, and
at zero the quantum is not a number at negative exponents at all. Nothing downstream is written to mean
anything there, and the cancellation the additive identity turns on is not defined there.

**A quantum law ranges over at least one magnitude.** A law over none describes no values, so the set
it parameterises is empty, and an empty set contains nothing, the additive identity included. This is
the sentence the trait's own documentation already carried as a request.

**A format declares a nonzero phase denominator.** A denominator of zero names no position on the
grid, so the phase coordinate does not denote and the predicate has nothing to be asked about.

Each fires for the same reason and with the same limit: a const, so it is evaluated at the verbs that
ask for it and at no other. Asking for it means carrying the force site rather than reaching one
through a call, and the distinction is what makes the sets comparable at all, because a set counted
transitively is not one set. In a const evaluation the branches are taken or not taken, so
`has_additive_identity` reaches the slot range's obligation for a format whose phase cancels and not
for one whose phase does not, and there the reach is a property of the declaration as much as of the
verb. At a runtime call it is neither: monomorphising the verb instantiates everything it calls, so
every obligation below it is forced for every declaration, whichever branch would have run.

Under that reading the format's obligation is forced at two verbs, `cancelling_slot` and
`has_additive_identity`, and a verb belongs to as many sets as it has force sites. Each of those two
carries a second site on the adjacent line, the ambient's and the quantum's respectively, so the sets
overlap by construction and the relation between them is not disjointness. What a guarantee is over is
its own set of verbs, and the section above names the routes that reach a value without meeting one.

**So the predicates stay total anyway**, and this is the half that does not depend on where the
obligations fire. Every function here has a defined answer for a declaration that violates one: an
empty magnitude range carries no identity, and a zero denominator names no position and so carries
none either. **That totality is the whole of what makes the answer defined**, rather than a second
line behind an obligation that would otherwise have removed the case, because the routes above are
exactly the cases no obligation is asked about.

**A quantum law reaches an exponent it can name.** The step law at the largest admitted magnitude has to
land inside what an `Exponent` carries, or the law names no quantum there, and a rate and a magnitude
count that between them run past it describe nothing at the magnitudes they claim. So the quantum
contract carries two conditions rather than one, and the reach is checked one domain wider than the
coordinates so that widening any of the three does not silently break the narrower form.

**Each obligation names what it names and nothing further.** None of them relates one contract to
another: a slot range that no format uses, a domain whose radix no quantum law exercises, and a phase
whose cancelling slot no range holds are each admissible on their own terms, and the predicates are
where those meet.

**The verdict-returning form is what a test uses.** `is_admissible_ambient`, `is_admissible_quantum`
and `is_admissible_format` ask the same question without forcing the const that would refuse it, which
is what lets a construction that compiles and is wrong live permanently in the test file instead of in
a scratch file somebody deletes.

## The platform-width points, which are ordinary points at a width the target supplies

A platform-width type is, at any one compilation, an ordinary member of the format inventory: quantum
one, phase zero, one magnitude, and a slot range `0 ..= 2^W - 1` or its two's complement twin, with the
width `W` bound to the compilation target's pointer width rather than written as a literal. Across
compilations the one name denotes a family of formats indexed by the target.

So `points` carries two aliases and no new type:

```
USize = UFixed<{ ::core::primitive::usize::BITS }, 0>
ISize = Integer<{ ::core::primitive::usize::BITS }>
```

An alias, because within one compilation the point is indistinguishable from a literal declaration of
the same width. A new struct would be distinguishable by name from `UFixed<64, 0>` at a 64-bit target,
and nothing through the `Format` contract could ever observe the difference, so the struct would carry a
distinction the concept does not have. `usize::BITS` is the width because it is the pointer width by
definition and it resolves before monomorphisation, on the format side of the line between a constant of
the type and a value that depends on a sibling datum. Spelled from `::core::primitive`, with the leading
`::`, rather than the bare name, because a bare `usize` resolves through whatever is in scope, and an item
named `usize` in the same module would take precedence over the primitive type there. A bare `core` is the
same failure one level up: a module, an import or an `extern crate` declared under that name in the same
crate would take precedence over the crate root there too. The leading `::` roots the path at the crate
graph rather than at whatever is in scope, which closes the first two: a module and an import named
`core` bind only in the module that declares them, and a leading-`::` path does not resolve among that
module's names. It does not close the third. An `extern crate ... as core` at the crate root, or a
dependency renamed to `core` in the manifest, binds the name among the crates, which reaches even a
leading-`::` path. The lint that guards the spelling refuses three source forms that bind the name
`core` in this crate, a module, a `use` item and an `extern crate` alias, and it reads nothing else, so
a manifest rename, a glob import, another kind of item named `core` and a macro-expanded form are not
refused by it. What the path alone rules out, and what the lint additionally rules out, are named
separately in "The laws the platform-width points owe tests for" below.

No `cfg` selects it, and no target is assumed. The width is read, not chosen per platform, so the
alias is written once and denotes the 16-, 32- or 64-bit point wherever it is compiled, all three of
which the ladder admits. A target whose pointer width the ladder did not admit would get the slot range's
own trait-bound refusal where the alias is used, with the ladder's diagnostic, rather than a format that
is wrong.

The unsigned point meets the range the platform-sized obligation names at 32 and 64 bits and not at
16. A unix errno is at most 4095 and a Windows GetLastError value is a 32-bit unsigned word, so both
are members at a pointer width of 32 or more, and at 16 the second is not. That is a fact about the
16-bit targets, stated so nobody reads the obligation as met there.

What this delivers is the format and not a value. The obligation asks for a number usable in a public
error type, and a format is a declared signature that holds none. No crate here holds a value of any
format yet, and what one datum of a numeral stands for is an open question, so the value half is not
built here and is not answered by this section.

No capacity type is built here. The platform-width ruling puts one outside its own scope, and the
question it answers says a capacity is the const generic position the primitive obligation excepts.
The ruling's note reads the numeric door's ruling as answering a capacity as a coordinate the door
carries; the door ruling's own text names no capacity, so whether a capacity is a position the door
carries is not settled by it. No row says what type spells a capacity. The obligation excepts a const
generic parameter from the stack's own types, and even there the bare form is used only where the
alternative is genuinely painful, and no row says whether a capacity is such a case. So a capacity is a
type-level parameter here and its spelling, the host's `usize` or a type the stack owns, is open, and
this design answers neither. The one fact that bears on it here is mechanical: a stack type at a const
generic position needs `adt_const_params`, which this crate does not enable, and
`an_arvo_type_as_a_const_parameter_is_refused` pins that refusal for `Width`.

### The laws the platform-width points owe tests for

Each point is the literal point at the target's width, through the contract: the slot range's width,
both ends, the count, the quantum, the phase and the magnitude count all read back equal to those of
`UFixed<W, 0>` and `Integer<W>` at `W = ::core::primitive::usize::BITS`, and the ends equal the host's own
`usize` and `isize` bounds.

The family is asserted at every pointer width, not at the one the suite happens to run on, since a
claim about a target-indexed family checked at one target is a claim about one member. What moves with
the width is the slot range, and the count and both ends of `Unsigned<W>` and `Signed<W>`, the ranges
`UFixed<W, 0>` and `Integer<W>` carry, are asserted at every admitted width on any host, 16, 32 and 64
among them. The error-word membership below is asserted over `UFixed<W, 0>` and `Integer<W>` at 16, 32
and 64. The quantum, the phase and the magnitude count do not depend on the width, and are read back
at the host's width only.

There is no arm per pointer width. Such an arm would assert that the alias equals the literal point at
the target's width, and that follows from the alias being spelled over `::core::primitive::usize::BITS`,
which the language defines as the pointer width: on every target it is true whenever the spelling is and
the leading-`::` path resolves to the real `core`. Whether it does is the shadowing question the lint's
second arm below answers, and its answer is narrower than every way the name `core` can be bound: a
`Cargo.toml` manifest rename of a dependency to the key `core` in particular is refused by nothing in this
crate. The one way the spelling itself fails is an
alias written against a literal width, and on the
host that is indistinguishable from the right spelling by anything a test reads, so no arm compiled for
the host could catch it. An arm written as a `#[test]` for another width only runs on a target of that
width. One written as a free `const _` item under a `cfg` for that width would be evaluated by any build
at that target, a check included; but this repository builds and checks at the host alone, so it would
guard nothing on the gate.

So the property is guarded where it lives, in the source text, by two lint arms rather than one. The lint
`the-platform-width-points-read-the-pointer-width` reads each alias's declaration up to its semicolon,
across lines, drops comments and whitespace, and compares what is left of the right-hand side exactly:
`UFixed<{::core::primitive::usize::BITS},0>` for `USize` and `Integer<{::core::primitive::usize::BITS}>`
for `ISize`. Anything else is a finding, a literal width, a width read from something other than
`::core::primitive::usize::BITS`, an expression around it and either alias over the other one's family
among them, and so is either alias missing.

Beside it, the same lint refuses three forms that bind the name `core`, declared anywhere in this crate.
It reads them as tokens once comments and literals are stripped, so whitespace, a line break or a comment
between two words changes nothing, and the raw identifier `r#core` counts as `core` in each form. The
first is a module named `core`. The second is a `use` item binding the name `core` anywhere in its tree:
an alias `as core`, an unaliased last segment `core` inside braces or not, or a `self` under a `core`
segment. The third is an `extern crate` item aliased `as core`.

The leading `::` resists, on its own, a module named `core`, a `use` alias `as core`, an unaliased `use`
whose last segment is `core` outside braces, and a `self` under a `core` segment. A module and a `use`
bind a name in the module that declares them, and a leading-`::` path resolves among the crates rather
than among that module's names, so each of these shadows only the bare spelling `core`, and only in the
module that holds it. They are refused because another path in that module may be spelled bare. The
lint also refuses an unaliased `core` inside braces beside other names, which the list of what the
leading `::` resists does not cover. The third is the one that reaches the
leading-`::` path: an `extern crate self as core` at the crate root makes `::core` name this crate
itself. A macro expanding to `extern crate self as core` is refused by the compiler already. A
`Cargo.toml` dependency renamed to the key `core` reaches `::core` the way the third form does. For a
`no_std` dependent, which is what this crate is, that takes as long as the path the edition's prelude
import names, `core::prelude::rust_2024` at edition 2024, resolves in the renamed crate, the exact path
rather than any `prelude` module: a `no_std` crate's own prelude is imported through the name `core`,
so the renamed crate is read for it. It needs nothing else of the real `core`: a renamed crate
re-exporting the real `core` whole, one re-exporting only its `prelude` module, and one holding an empty
hand-written `prelude::rust_2024` each let `::core::primitive::usize::BITS` read the renamed crate's
value, and one holding a `prelude` module with nothing in it, no `rust_2024` submodule, is refused at
