time the same way the `no_std` dependents' own checks are. This lint reads source only and never sees a
manifest, so that rename is unguarded.

Both arms run on every commit with no second build. What neither guards is anything beyond the two
declarations and the three forms: the family laws are the tests', and a glob import bringing in an item
named `core`, another kind of item named `core`, a macro expanding to one of the forms, a manifest rename,
or a third platform-width alias would each need the lint extended to name it.

The host tests read the aliases against `usize::BITS`, `usize::MAX` and `isize::MIN`, and the slot
range laws at 16, 32 and 64 run on the host. With the spelling fixed exactly, rooted at the crate graph
by the leading `::`, and with the lint refusing the three forms above, that covers the pointer widths a
target has, 16, 32 and 64, against a shadow declared in this crate's source through one of those three
forms. A shadow reached some other way, a manifest rename foremost, is not covered by anything in this
crate.

The obligation's range is a membership question, asked in both directions: the errno ceiling and the
GetLastError ceiling are members at 32 and 64, and the second is not at 16.

## Where the exact step happens, which is not in the set it lands in

The factoring says arithmetic on a format is an exact operation in an ambient domain composed
with a named total adaptation onto the representable set. **Those are two acts in two places**, and
reading them as one is how a map ends up computing the exact step in the carrier its result has to fit
rather than in one wide enough to hold the step.

So the map performs the exact step in a domain chosen to hold it, and the adaptation lands the result
in the representable set. The step's intermediate is not a slot index and is not required to be one;
only the answer is.

That is also why the intermediate is not a coordinate and carries no coordinate type. For rounding it
is the slot at or below the position and one bit saying whether to step above it, a private pair
inside the crate's own functions that names one more position than a `Slot` does, on purpose, and
giving it a name in the surface would put a quantity on the public API that nothing outside has any
business holding.

**The same reading covers the slot that cancels a phase.** Working out where zero would sit divides an
offset a caller wrote by its own denominator and negates it, which leaves the range a slot index is
carried in at one nameable pair, so it happens in a domain wide enough to hold it and inside the
function that asks. Nothing about it reaches the surface: what comes back is a `Bool`.

**This is what makes the map total without a check.** Wrapping a very large exact position into a small
range has a correct answer, and the only reason a design would refuse or guard there is that it chose
a carrier too narrow for the intermediate. Carrying the intermediate in a form that holds every
position the step can reach removes the case rather than catching it, which is what the design asks
for: no check on the value path, and no arm that only holds under a build flag. For rounding that form
is the pair above rather than a wider integer, since the step adds at most one slot and one bit is
all the extra reach it needs.

A guard would have been the wrong answer twice over. Under `overflow-checks` it is a runtime check the
canon forbids; without it, it is nothing at all, and the wrong value it lets through lands **inside**
the representable range where no consumer can tell it from a right one.

### The two regions are internal

The map is one map with two regions and not two mechanisms, so the regions are not two functions a
consumer selects between. `adapt` is the surface; rounding and completion are how it is written.

Publishing them separately invites the use the sentence above says is not the shape, and it is what put
a range on the public surface as loose parameters rather than as the declared thing it comes from.

### The adaptation forces the admission obligation

`adapt` reads a slot range from the declared signature. Reading an associated const does not force the
contract's obligation on its own, so `adapt` forces it, and the range the completion works over is one
that has been admitted rather than one that merely arrived.

## The industrial conventions, as instances of the open inventory

The standards bound starts at two and reserves the rest. MATLAB `fi` and `fimath` and IEEE 754 are the
two the parity suite is built against, with SystemC `sc_fixed`, Vitis `ap_fixed` and Algorithmic C
`ac_fixed` reserved rather than excluded. A convention is a naming over what this crate
already carries, so it joins the same way any instance does and nothing about the concept grows.

**Nothing behind the naming is the test of whether it is honest.** A convention that needed this crate
to compute something to make it work would not be a convention arvo supports badly; it would be a gap
in the primitives, which is what the standards bound is there to find: every operation arvo declares
is a function of the declared width. So the interesting output of this section is its refusals, and
both of them below are claims about this crate rather than about MATLAB.

### The coordinate map, which is five names for things that exist

A fi carries a signedness, a word length and a fraction length, and its real-world value is the stored
integer divided by two to the power of the fraction length. A fimath carries a rounding method and an
overflow action.

| What MATLAB calls it | What carries it |
|---|---|
| signedness | `Fi` for signed and `Ufi` for unsigned, which is the choice of slot range |
| word length | the width parameter of `Fi` or `Ufi` |
| fraction length | `FractionLength`, a quantum law whose exponent is its negation |
| rounding method | `Rounding` |
| overflow action | `Overflow` |

`FiMath` and `UfiMath` name the declared signature a fi and a fimath make together, because a
convention's math settings are part of the signature rather than an argument: two settings over one
format are two declared signatures.

MATLAB's property names stay unbackticked. Backticks here mean an item in this source tree, and four
of these are another system's vocabulary that never will be.

The negation is the one place the spelling fights. Written directly it is an expression in a const
generic argument, which needs a feature this workspace forbids. Naming what the expression computes
dissolves it: the fraction length is a quantum law of its own, its exponent is that negation computed
in an impl body where an arbitrary expression is legal, and the alias bounds on the `Quantum` contract
instead. A negative fraction length is ordinary in the convention and falls out with no case of its
own, since it is the same negation landing on a positive exponent.

### The overflow map is total and the rounding map is not

Wrap and Saturate are the two overflow actions and both are shipped policies.

Five of the six rounding methods land on a shipped mode. Ceiling is `Ceil`, Floor is `Floor`, Zero is
`TowardZero`, Convergent is `HalfEven`, and Nearest is `HalfUp`: MathWorks documents Nearest as ties
toward positive infinity, which is what `half_up` denotes.

Round does not. MathWorks documents Round as ties toward the greater absolute value, which is ties
away from zero, and that is not one of the six. The canon reaches it as the alias the adaptation
section names, a shift composed with Zero, so a fimath naming Round has no rounding mode to name, and
the alias has no spelling in this crate yet, for the reason under the open items. It is marked and not
asserted, since an arm claiming a mode for Round would be the seventh name the vocabulary is closed
against.

Which shipped mode is Nearest is measured rather than read off the re-export. Four positions decide
whether a mode is nearest with ties toward positive infinity, two off the grid and a tie of each sign,
and exactly one of the six passes. The same four with the negative tie read the other way decide
whether a mode is Round, and none of the six passes, which is the alias having no mode.

### The width ladder stops far short of the convention's

MathWorks documents the word length from 1 to 65535 unsigned and 2 to 65535 signed. The slot range is
implemented at widths 1 to 64, the bound the slot range section gives its reasons for.

So a fi at word length 65 has no format, and neither has anything wider, which covers most of the word
lengths the convention documents. `Fi` and `Ufi` carry the slot range's bound, so it fails to build where the type is named,
with the diagnostic the slot range already supplies, and the compile-fail case is where that is
recorded so it cannot be lost.

Worth saying what this is not. No operation reads a carrier, so nothing here contradicts behaviour
being stated per declared signature. What is bounded is the vocabulary of widths that can be declared
at all.

### What a parity arm asserts, and what a published number is

The standard's own documented answer, against what this crate returns. An arm computing both sides
here would assert that the crate agrees with itself.

**A printed number is not the value.** MathWorks prints five significant figures, so four of its five
worked examples print a rounding of the real-world value. What an arm asserts is the stored integer
exactly, and that the value rounds to what is printed, both in exact integers so no float sits between
the two sides.

The five arms are asserted under both nearest modes at once, which is honest only because pi times a
power of two is never a tie, so none of them reaches a tie rule. Nearest is `HalfUp` and Convergent is
`HalfEven`, and the two differ only at a tie, so an arm asserting one stored integer for both is
asserting each method's documented answer. A control asserts that no arm reaches a tie, so the day one
of them does, the arm fails rather than asserting one number for two methods that disagree there.

## The two symmetries of the grid, and where the map commutes with them

A grid is a set of points spaced one quantum apart, and it has two symmetries. Translating by a whole
number of quanta carries the grid onto itself, and so does reflecting it through zero. The adaptation
is a map onto that grid, so for each symmetry there is a question about whether the map commutes with
it, and each has an answer that holds in one region and fails outside it.

The canon names the first of the two.
`law::fusing_a_multiply_add_preserves_the_answer_under_unsigned` and
`law::fusing_a_multiply_add_preserves_the_answer_under_signed_wrapping` state in their own sentences
that the property they are about is translation equivariance of the rounding position on the domain
the cell reaches. The list of laws the applied map owes tests for, above, did not carry it, which left
a law stated one tier up with no expression here and no test against it.

Their mode sets and this crate's do not correspond one for one. Both rows name `away from zero`, which
is not one of the six names the vocabulary carries and which the canon reaches as an alias of
`toward_zero`, and neither row names a stochastic decision, which this crate ships. So what the region
below claims is over the six this crate ships, and the five those two sets share are where the two can
be compared at all. They agree at all five on both rows. On the unsigned row, where no position is
negative, floor, ceil, toward zero and half-up commute and half-even does not. On the signed wrapping
row floor, ceil and half-up commute and toward zero and half-even do not, and the region below says the
same of a domain reaching a negative position and a tie, which the row's failing region for half-even
shows the cell reaches.

The reason it belongs here rather than wherever a multiply-add eventually lives is that the law is
about the map. Take an exact position, translate it by a representable amount, and ask whether
adapting the translated position gives the slot that adapting and then translating gives:

```
relocation:  adapt(position + c) == adapt(adapt(position) + c)
reflection:  adapt(-position)    == adapt(-adapt(position))
```

Neither quantifies over an operation. Relocation quantifies over every exact position and every
representable translation, so a fused multiply-add is the case where the position is a product and the
translation is the addend, and the operation decides which region of the law it sits in and decides
nothing else. Both are written with the right side re-adapting because the translate or the negation
of an admitted slot need not itself be admitted: a two's complement range carries one more slot below
zero than above it, so the negation of its lowest slot is not a slot.

Each law decomposes into one property per region of the map, and the conjunction is the whole of it.
That is why the two regions being separable is load-bearing rather than descriptive: it is what makes
a law about the composition answerable as two independent questions.

### The rounding region, and what a mode reads

A position is a slot and a residue in the unit interval. Translating moves the slot and leaves the
residue alone, so a rule that decides between the two neighbours by reading only the residue returns
the same offset at every slot and commutes by construction. A rule reading anything else commutes only
where the thing it reads does not vary over the domain.

So the region is decided by two facts about a mode: what it reads besides the residue, and when it
reads it. Over the six names the vocabulary carries there are three answers. Floor, ceil, half-up and
stochastic read nothing besides the residue: half-up adds half a step and floors, and whether a half
added to the residue reaches the next slot does not depend on which slot it is. Toward zero reads the
sign of the slot, at every position off the grid. Half-even reads the parity of the slot, at a tie
only. A rule reading the sign at a tie only is a cell of this classification that no shipped name
occupies; ties away from zero is that rule, and the suite keeps it planted so the derivation is shown
to reach the cell.

From those two facts the region follows without a table per mode. The rounding region commutes with
translation where the mode reads nothing, or where it reads only at a tie and the domain reaches no
tie, or where what it reads is the sign and the domain reaches no negative position, or where no
position is off the grid at all, because then the region is never entered and there is nothing for any
mode to read. Parity is the one
the sign restriction does not defeat, and it is defeated instead by a domain whose translations are
all even. That last region is named and not expressed: the reach a caller hands the predicate carries
no parity of the translation band, so the predicate answers no on a domain where the law holds. It is
conservative rather than unsound, and it is a real region an arm could gate on once the declared
signature carries the fact.

The domain the mode is asked about is the positions together with the translated positions, not the
positions alone. A domain of non-negative positions with a translation that may be negative reaches a
negative position, so a region stated over the declared positions would license a mode that reads the
sign where it does not hold.

### The completion region, and what a policy throws away

Wrapping is reduction modulo the span, which is a homomorphism of the additive group, so it commutes
with translation whatever the range and whatever the values. A clamp pins a value to a bound and
throws away how far past it the value went, and the law fails exactly where a later translation would
have given that distance back.

So a clamp commutes with translation where no translation can point back at the range from the side an
excursion left on. On a range whose lowest slot is at or above zero every representable translation is
non-negative, so no excursion above the top can be undone, and an excursion below the bottom still
can. The region is therefore about which excursion sides the values reach crossed with which signs the
translations carry, and stating it as unsigned would be wrong in the direction that licenses a wrong
answer.

### The reflection, and the partition

The same decomposition at the other symmetry. The rounding region commutes with reflection for toward
zero and half-even, and not for floor, ceil, half-up or a stochastic decision at a fixed dither.
Half-up fails at a tie and nowhere else: adding half a step floors a tie up at either sign, so a tie and
its negation go to slots that are not each other's negation. The completion region commutes with
reflection always under wrapping, because negation is an automorphism of the cyclic group the
reduction lands in, and under a clamp where the range is symmetric about zero. A two's complement range
is not symmetric, and no shipped format is, so the clamp's reflection region is reached only by a
declared range that an outside implementor supplies.

Over the six names the vocabulary carries, the two symmetries partition: on a domain reaching a
negative position and a tie every mode commutes with exactly one of them, none with both and none with
neither. Four commute with translation, floor, ceil, half-up and stochastic, and two with reflection,
toward zero and half-even. One half of that is a theorem for a rule that meets a tie: a rule reading
only the residue sends the residues `r` and `1 - r` to steps that have to sum to one for reflection to
hold, and the midpoint, which is its own mirror, cannot. The other half is measured over these six
rather than derived, because a rule reading the sign need not commute with reflection. So the
reflection fact is carried per name and checked, rather than derived from what the mode reads.

Half-up does commute with reflection on a domain that reaches no tie, and every mode does on a domain
entirely on the grid. The reflection predicate takes a mode and no reach, so it refuses both regions.
That is conservative rather than unsound, the same shape as the parity region above, and expressing it
wants the reflection predicate to read a reach. It is named and not expressed here, because the
stochastic mode's reflection region at a fixed dither depends on the dither, which a reach does not
carry.

### The reach, which is the half a declared signature cannot supply

A declared signature says which slots are admitted. It does not say which exact positions an operation
produces before the adaptation sees them, which translations that operation applies, or whether an
exactly-half position is among them, and all three decide a region above.

That is what `Reach` carries. It is a coordinate rather than a set of loose arguments because the six
questions the two laws ask of it are derived from it rather than declared beside it: whether any
position is off the grid, whether a negative position is reached, whether a negative or a positive
translation is reached, and whether a rounded value can sit below the lowest admitted slot or above the
highest. The last two are derived from the position bounds because the rounding region returns the
position's own slot or the one above it and never anything else, which is a law this section owes a
test for.

Whether a position is off the grid is one coordinate with three values rather than two flags:
every position on the grid, some off it with no exactly-half one among them, or an exactly-half one
reached. An on-grid reach with a tie in it would be a contradiction, and a coordinate of three values is
what makes it one nobody can write. On the grid the rounding region returns the position itself, so a
rounded value sits above the highest slot only where the position does, and the upper excursion is
strict there rather than inclusive. Without that, a reach whose highest position is the highest slot
reads as an excursion that never happens, and a relocation the map honours is refused.

A consumer that knows none of it declares `Reach::EVERYTHING`, which licenses only the cells where
both regions commute whatever the reach. The conservative answer is the default because the cost of
being wrong is not symmetric: refusing a law that holds costs a lowering, and licensing one that does
not costs a wrong result.

### The laws this section owes tests for

Each is stated over what the map computes rather than over what a declaration says, and each can be
wrong about arithmetic.

The map relocates where the predicate says it does, and does not where it says it does not. Asserted
over the cross of every mode, every shipped policy, a range reaching negatives, a range reaching none
and a range symmetric about zero, and every combination of the restrictions the reach carries. The
test that matters is the one that would fail if the predicate licensed a law the map refuses, so the
two directions are counted separately: a licensed law the map refuses is a defect, and a refused law
the map honours is a cost.

An on-grid reach is among those combinations, and there the rounding half holds for every mode and the
map agrees, including where the highest position is the highest slot, which the inclusive bound would
have refused.

The map reflects where the predicate says it does, over the same cross.

The classification of a mode agrees with the map. The test derives what a mode reads by walking the
map over domains that differ in one restriction at a time, and asserts the shipped classification
equals what it derived. This is what stops the two laws above from resting on a declaration: a wrong
classification fails here rather than being confirmed by a predicate that restates it. The derivation
is also run over the planted rule that reads the sign at a tie only, which lands in the one cell no
shipped name occupies, so every cell the classification has is shown reachable by the instrument.

The rounded slot is the position's own slot or the one above it, over every mode. The reach's
derivation of the excursion sides rests on that bound, so without it the derivation would be unsound
rather than conservative.

The two symmetries partition the six shipped names on a domain reaching a negative position and a tie,
four and two. Stated as a law because it is a measured fact that a seventh name could break, and a
name is exactly what a design change can add. It is asserted per mode rather than as a count:
translation by one step, by odd and by even multiples of it, across the sign and on one side of it, and
negation, each with the modes it holds for and the modes it fails for, and each failure at a named
witness.

And the sweeps that establish any of the above reach both answers, reach a tie, reach a negative
position, and reach a value outside the range on both sides. A sweep that entered one region only
would confirm every arm in it while establishing nothing, and the counts are asserted rather than
assumed.

### The surface this section names

`Reach` is the region coordinate: `Reach::of` takes the positions an operation reaches,
`translated_by` adds the translations it applies, `without_ties` says no exactly-half position is
among them, `on_grid` says no position is off the grid at all, and `Reach::EVERYTHING` is the
conservative declaration. `reaches_off_the_grid` and `reaches_a_tie` read the grid state back.

`Reads`, `When` and `Behaviour` are what a mode does besides the residue, and `behaviour_of` is the
classification. They are named rather than hidden inside a predicate because they are what makes the
regions one rule instead of a table with a row per mode, and because the law asserting the
classification against the map needs something to assert about.

`rounding_is_translation_equivariant` takes a `Mode` and a `Reach`.
`completion_is_translation_homomorphic` takes a declared signature and a `Reach`, because it reads the
range. `adaptation_relocates` is the conjunction. `rounding_is_reflection_equivariant`,
`completion_is_reflection_equivariant` and `adaptation_reflects` are the same three at the other
symmetry. Every one returns a `Bool` and every one is a `const fn` over associated items fixed at
monomorphisation, so a consumer gating an arm on one has no branch left by the time the backend sees
it.

### Why this is not the predicate this crate deleted

`overflow` and `rounding` each carry a note saying two predicates stood there and were deleted rather
than improved, because each was a `matches!` over an enumeration that no code read, so their tests
reached a declaration and stopped. That note is right and it is the first thing to check anything new
against.

Two things separate what this section adds. A predicate here takes a region, so its answer is a
function of six facts about a domain rather than a restatement of one enumeration, and each of those
six was shown to move a verdict rather than assumed to. And the test is a reader that runs the
machinery: it derives the classification by walking the map and asserts the shipped one equals it,
which is the repair this design already prescribes for an assertion that reaches a declaration and
stops.

Addition reads them. Its associativity verdict is the completion half of the relocation law read at the
reach addition supplies, and the rounding half decides whether that reading applies at all, so the
conjunction is written once, here, and the operation gates on it rather than writing it again.

## Addition, the one operation this crate admits

The admission rule asks one thing of an operation, that it be a function of the declared signature,
and addition is the operation whose exact step shows most plainly what that means. It reads the phase
and the slot range and nothing else, so it can be stated here, in the crate that cannot reach a
carrier, and it could not be stated in any crate that can.

It is also what two ratified laws were about before anything computed it. The additive verdict and
absorption both speak of an addition reduced onto the representable set, and the relocation predicates
above were written for an operation to read. This section is that operation.

### The exact step names no quantum

Two members of a format are `(phase + a) * q` and `(phase + b) * q` at one quantum `q`, so their exact
sum is

```
(phase + a) * q + (phase + b) * q = (phase + (a + b + phase)) * q
```

which is the member at position `a + b + phase`, counted in slots. The whole part of the phase adds to
the slot and the fractional part is the remainder the rounding region reads. Neither the quantum nor the
radix appears, which is the additive verdict's independence of the fraction width stated as a property
of the arithmetic: addition at a common scale never reads the scale, so a verdict about it cannot move
with the scale. The tests that assert it run a decimal format beside the binary ones rather than
trusting the argument.

`sum_position` computes that position in the slot index's own host integer and returns an `Exact`. A
phase is carried as the pair it was declared with, so the whole part is a euclidean division of the pair
after the sign has moved onto the numerator, taken in that integer, which is one domain wider than the
pair, for the same reason the phase's other readers take it there.

The operands are slots, and nothing about a slot says it is a member, since the value type that would
carry membership is the one this crate does not ship. The carry obligation bounds the sum of two
members, so for members the sum is exact. For a slot outside the range there is no member sum to be
exact about, and the step still has to return a position, so the sum saturates at the ends of the
index's host integer, the same act `Reach` performs on a bound that leaves it. That keeps `add` total
over every pair of slots. Under saturation and clamping the answer is then the one the true sum would
get; under wrapping it is not, once the sum of two non-members leaves that integer, and that is the whole of what
the saturation costs.

### What it refuses, and where the refusal is seen

The step is stated over one quantum, and that is the first thing it refuses without. In the
magnitude-indexed family a slot names a different value at each magnitude, so two members may sit at
two quanta and the sum may belong at a third. The applied map has no magnitude coordinate to land it at:
an `Exact` is a slot and a remainder at the one magnitude a constant-quantum format has. So a format
whose quantum is not the constant family is refused, and the refusal names that coordinate as what is
missing.

The second refusal is a sum the slot coordinate cannot carry. The exact positions addition produces
span `2 * MIN + phase` to `2 * MAX + phase`, and a position outside what a `Slot` holds is not a
position. No format this crate ships reaches it: the widest slot range is 64 bits and a phase is a
signed 64-bit pair, so every sum of two members is more than sixty bits inside the index's host integer.
An outside range placed near either end of that integer does reach it, and the refusal is what stops the step
leaving the coordinate silently. The condition is computed with checked arithmetic, since the sum it
asks about is exactly the one that may not fit.

The third is a remainder the position cannot hold. An `Exact` carries its remainder as a `Fraction`,
whose denominator is a positive value the coordinate carries, and the phase's fractional part needs the
phase's reduced denominator there. Two kinds of declared pair reach past that. A denominator of zero
names no remainder at all; the format's own obligation refuses it where that obligation is forced, and
addition's obligation carries the condition itself rather than forcing the format's, so the format's
obligation keeps exactly the two force sites the section on obligations names. And a denominator of the
least value with an odd numerator has a reduced denominator of two to the sixty-third. `Fraction::of`
saturates such a ratio, which is its own second region and close enough for a ratio, and at the largest
odd numerator it saturates to exactly one, which `Exact::between` then carries into the slot. The
position would land a whole slot above the sum, so the saturation that is harmless for a ratio is wrong
for a position, and refusing is the only answer that keeps the step exact.

All three are one obligation. `sum_position` forces it, so everything that adds does, and
`addition_reach` and `addition_is_associative` force it as well, because they compute the same
positions and would otherwise saturate them for a format whose sum leaves the coordinate.
It is a const, so it has the reach every obligation in this crate has: a runtime call meets it when a
binary is produced, and a call bound in a `const` item meets it at check time, which is the form the
compile-fail cases pin with their diagnostics. `is_addable` asks the same question without forcing it,
which is what lets a test hold a format addition refuses and report on it.

### The operation, and what reads its arity

`Add` is the operation: it names the declared signature it is a function of and its arity, two. It
carries nothing else, because the admission rule asks for nothing else. The arity is read rather than
decorative: the reach addition supplies is computed from it, so an arity that disagreed with the step
would move the reach away from the positions the step produces, and the test comparing the two would
say so.

`add` is the composition the factoring names: `sum_position`, then `adapt` under the same signature,
with the dither passed through for the one mode that reads it. It takes two slots and returns one. It
carries no check and does not panic; the panic section above says where a caller asks instead.

### Where the adapted sum is associative

The canon decides this by absorption: the reduction is associative exactly when reducing a reachable
exact value before combining it with a stored operand does not change the adapted result, quantified
over the values the format holds. For addition that is the relocation law above, read at the positions
addition produces and the translations its second step applies, and quantified over stored operands
rather than over the ambient domain. The ambient quantifier is a different and stronger predicate, and
it is the one that refuses associative cases.

`addition_reach` is the reach addition supplies: positions from `2 * MIN + k` to `2 * MAX + k`, where
`k` is the phase's whole part, with the grid state its fractional part decides. On the grid when the
phase is whole, at a tie when the fractional part is one half, and off the grid otherwise. It carries no
translation, because at a fractional phase the second step's translation is a stored operand plus the
phase, which is not a whole number of slots and is not something `Reach` can hold.

`addition_is_associative` answers from that reach in three cases.

When the phase is whole every position is on the grid, the rounding region is never entered, and the
verdict is the completion half of the relocation law at the positions above with translations from
`MIN + k` to `MAX + k`.

When the phase is fractional the rounding region is asked about every step. Where the rounding half of
the relocation law holds over the reach, rounding adds one fixed offset of zero or one slot at every
position, so addition is the whole-phase addition at `k` plus that offset, and the verdict is the
completion question there. The offset is read by rounding one position of the reach, since holding the
rounding half is exactly what makes every position give the same one.

The stochastic mode's offset is the dither's rather than the position's, so for it the verdict needs
the completion question to hold at both offsets. That is the verdict for every dither at once rather
than for one, which is the claim a caller that does not know its dither can use.

Where the rounding half does not hold over the reach, the offset varies from one position to the next
and addition is not a translated whole-phase addition. The verdict refuses there. That is a cost rather
than a defect, since some of those cells are associative anyway, and the tests count them rather than
assume there are none.

Over every cell the tests cross, a licensed cell never has a divergent triple. At a whole phase the
verdict is exact in both directions over any range of two slots or more: a range whose top is reachable
from above by a negative translation has the witness one past the top, and one reachable from below the
witness one past the bottom, so a refusal always names a triple that diverges. The tests assert both
directions, which reproduces the ratified biconditional over a wider set of ranges than it was measured
on, ranges that do not contain zero among them.

### What this section does not build

The magnitude-indexed family. Two things block it and they are different in kind. In the design, the
applied map takes a slot and a remainder at one magnitude, so a sum whose operands sit at two
magnitudes has no position to be handed as, and giving it one is a change to `Exact` and to `adapt`
rather than to addition. In the canon, the completion policies are stated over a representable range,
and a magnitude-indexed set is a union of ranges with no single span to reduce modulo. No row says what
wrapping means there, and `where_wrapping_lives` classifies wrapping rather than defining it on such a
set. Saturation and clamping have an evident reading on that set and wrapping does not, which is the
call this design does not make.

Addition across two signatures, a fold, and a numeral value type, all under what this crate does not
decide.

### The laws this section owes tests for

Each is stated over what the operation computes.

The exact step is the denotation. For every pair of slots at small widths, the value `sum_position`
names equals the exact rational sum of the two members' values, over quantum exponents on both sides of
zero, radix two and radix ten, and whole, half and third phases. A step that adds the phase in value
units rather than slot units, which is what a design reading the scale would compute, is kept as a
negative control and disagrees.

Addition is total: every pair at every admitted width of both slot families, at the edges of the range,
maps to an admitted slot, over every mode and every policy, and so does every pair of slots at the ends
of the coordinate, outside every range, where under saturation the answer is also the one the true sum
gets.

Addition is commutative, over every pair, mode, policy and phase the sweeps reach.

Addition is exact where it can be: when the exact position is on the grid and inside the range, the
answer is that slot, and where the format carries an additive identity, adding the identity's slot
returns the other operand unchanged.

The verdict and the counts are independent of the fraction width: the divergent-triple count at four
bits is the same at every quantum exponent from minus eight to eight and at radix ten, and so is the
verdict.

At a whole phase the answer is the same under every rounding mode, which answers the ratified additive
row's missing rounding axis where it can be answered: nothing rounds there. At a fractional phase two
modes are shown to disagree, so the rounding axis is real wherever the phase is not whole.

The counts the canon records are reproduced: 952 divergent triples at four bits signed under
saturation, none under wrapping, none unsigned, and 4,177,792 at eight bits.

The verdict agrees with brute force. Over every shipped point at the widths the sweep can afford, over
a family of test-local slot ranges covering every interval of small bounds whether it contains zero or
not, over whole and fractional phases, and over every mode, every policy and every dither the sweep
carries, a licensed cell never diverges, both answers are reached, and the refused cells that are
associative anyway are counted and reported rather than hidden. Two negative controls go with it: a
rule keyed on signedness disagrees with brute force on a signed range that is associative, and the
relocation predicate asked over ambient translations beyond the format refuses a format that is
associative, which is the quantifier the canon warns about.

The reach addition supplies is the reach the step produces: its bounds are the least and greatest slot
`sum_position` returns over every pair, and its grid state is the one the returned remainders show.

What must not compile does not: addition over a magnitude-indexed format, over an outside format whose
sum is not carried, over an outside format whose phase remainder the position cannot hold, over a
format that is not a declared signature, and with a host integer where a
slot goes. Each is a compile-fail case with its diagnostic committed.

### The surface this section names

`Add` is the operation. `sum_position` is the exact step, `add` the composition, and `is_addable` the
verdict beside the obligation. `addition_reach` is the reach addition supplies and
`addition_is_associative` the verdict on where the adapted sum is associative. Every one is a
`const fn` over associated items or a type, so a consumer gating an arm on the verdict has no branch
left by the time the backend sees it.

## Open items

Three gaps this crate does not close, catalogued here rather than left unrecorded once the design
that would close them exists.

`Exact` carries the distance a position lies past an end of the slot index, `past`, and nothing in this
crate's public surface reads it. A consumer holding an `Exact` from a public constructor has no way to
ask how far past the index it sits, which is not a defect against any obligation stated so far and is
worth a design decision once a consumer wants that distance, rather than being decided as a side effect
of a change made for something else.

`sum_position` saturates the exact step at the ends of the slot index's own integer when an operand is
a slot the format does not admit, which the doc comment on `sum_position` states. What is open is
whether saturating there, rather than carrying the true exact position the way it does for two members,
is the right answer for a caller supplying a non-member slot, or whether addition should refuse such a
slot instead of computing a position for it. Not decided here.

The alias the canon names for ties away from zero, `toward_zero(x + sign(x) q/2)`, is a shift of the
exact position composed with the `toward_zero` adaptation, and this crate names no operation on an
exact position that forms the shift. `Exact` reads back its slot and whether it is on the grid or at a
tie, and not its remainder or its sign, so a caller holding an `Exact` it did not build, the one
`sum_position` returns among them, cannot form the shifted position at all. A caller that holds the
slot and the ratio it built the position from can form it through `Exact::between`, and only where
twice the ratio's denominator fits the ratio's own integer or the denominator is even, since the half
is added over that denominator. So the alias is expressible over part of the domain and by some
callers only, which is a gap in this design rather than a composition it provides. Where the operation
forming the shift lives, and whether it is exact over every position, is the design decision, and it
is not made here.
